ss=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np ps nr ns nt pt nv nw nx pu nz=
 oa ob pv od oe of pw oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The at=
tention mechanism has 3 inputs: the <strong class=3D"no hm">query</strong>,=
 the <strong class=3D"no hm">keys</strong>, and the <strong class=3D"no hm"=
>values</strong> (denoted as <strong class=3D"no hm">Q, K,</strong> and <st=
rong class=3D"no hm">V</strong>, respectively). At this point, the meaning =
behind these names is not important. What <strong class=3D"no hm"><em class=
=3D"ol">is</em></strong> important, however, is where these values are comi=
ng from:</p><p id=3D"5b7b" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np=
 nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-se=
lectable-paragraph=3D""><mark class=3D"zh zi aq"><strong class=3D"no hm">Th=
e query, keys, and values are each a seperate linear transformation of the =
same exact input.</strong></mark></p><p id=3D"bdb9" class=3D"pw-post-body-p=
aragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of o=
g oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">This is known as <strong c=
lass=3D"no hm">self-attention</strong>, and is the bedrock of the Transform=
er architecture.</p><p id=3D"4787" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl=
 no b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl"=
 data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">So, in order to get (<strong class=3D"no hm=
">Q, K, V</strong>), we take our input (<strong class=3D"no hm">X</strong>)=
 and apply three seperate linear transformations to it. In practice, these =
=E2=80=9Clinear transformations=E2=80=9D consist of multiplying our input b=
y some weight matrix, <strong class=3D"no hm">W</strong>.</p><p id=3D"c8c4"=
 class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx n=
y nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">No=
w, in large language models, such as GPT, these inputs are usually the outp=
ut of an embedding layer. If you=E2=80=99re not familiar with what an embed=
ding layer is, you can think of it as a lookup table, where each word (or s=
ub-word) is mapped to a fixed-sized vector. Throughout the course of traini=
ng, the values resulting from this mapping are optimized, ultimately creati=
ng a rich representation of the underlying text.</p><p id=3D"04fd" class=3D=
"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa o=
b oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">As such, ta=
ke a look at the below (crude) code example, which processes and embeds a s=
entence by doing the following:</p><ol class=3D""><li id=3D"1412" class=3D"=
