ectable-paragraph=3D"">If these numbers are hard to comprehend, maybe this =
will help put things in perspective: It is estimated that it would take <st=
rong class=3D"no hm"><em class=3D"ol">355 years</em></strong> to <a class=
=3D"ah ok" href=3D"https://lambdalabs.com/blog/demystifying-gpt-3" rel=3D"n=
oopener ugc nofollow" target=3D"_blank">train GPT-3 on a single GPU</a>. So=
, if you started today, hopefully your great-great-great-great-great grandk=
id would be able to see it complete (assuming GPT-20 hasn=E2=80=99t rendere=
d humanity extinct by then=E2=80=A6kidding).</p><p id=3D"a042" class=3D"pw-=
post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc=
 od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">For each succes=
sive model, the increase in parameters is a direct result of the increase i=
n dataset size, which can be seen in the table above. Generally speaking, t=
he larger the dataset, the larger the model. And it is in this relationship=
 where we can begin to grasp why these GPT models are so powerful.</p><p id=
=3D"b7c6" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu n=
v nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-paragrap=
h=3D"">In short: As GPT=E2=80=99s dataset grows, it is becoming closer and =
closer to an ultra-compressed version of the Internet.</p><p id=3D"3e98" cl=
ass=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny n=
z oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Think=
 about it: when you want to know more about a topic, where do you go? Wikip=
edia? GPT is trained on Wikipedia. What about Google? Well, Google=E2=80=99=
s dataset is a privatized version of CommonCrawl =E2=80=94 which GPT is als=
o trained on.</p><p id=3D"74e7" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no=
 b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" da=
ta-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Do you see the theme here? GPT is not just a l=
anguage model. Rather, it is something more. In fact, it isn=E2=80=99t a st=
retch to say that GPT (and others like it) are human-knowledge models.</p><=
p id=3D"e9b9" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt =
nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-para=
graph=3D"">To elaborate, let=E2=80=99s think about what GPT is actually doi=
ng.</p><p id=3D"85c6" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq n=
r ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selecta=
ble-paragraph=3D"">GPT is trained to predict the next token in a sequence. =
On a simplistic level, you can think of these tokens as the characters or w=
ords in a body of text. By doing this over hundreds of millions of web page=
s (probably even <em class=3D"ol">billions</em> at this point), it is not o=
nly modeling language; but rather, it is also modeling <em class=3D"ol">ide=
as</em>.</p><p id=3D"9a01" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np=
 nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-se=
lectable-paragraph=3D"">After all, that is how we as humans express our ide=
as: through language. So, by training GPT on the very medium in which we ex=
press thought, it makes sense how it is ultimately able to capture our coll=
ective ideas. And by doing so, it has become capable of some truly mind-num=
bing things.</p><p id=3D"3715" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no =
b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" dat=
a-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Of course, this conversation always has the ten=
dency to gradually boil down to the question: <em class=3D"ol">What is inte=
lligence?</em> So, since we were bound to get there eventually, let=E2=80=
=99s just get on with it.</p><p id=3D"36b6" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph=
 nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi =
oj he bl" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Is ChatGPT or GPT-4 the first form=
 of Artificial General Intelligence (AGI)? How would we even know that? How=
 do we even measure intelligence? Better yet, <em class=3D"ol">what is</em>=
 intelligence? (<em class=3D"ol">queue the existential angst</em>)</p><p id=
=3D"b2a1" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu n=
v nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-paragrap=
h=3D"">I obviously don=E2=80=99t know the answer to that question, nor does=
 anyone. However, it is often useful to approach it like so: If you were to=
 train a true Artificial General Intelligence, how would you go about it? H=
ow would you teach it? And what would be the benchmark for success?</p><p i=
d=3D"8cf2" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu =
nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-paragra=
ph=3D"">It is in this vein that I would argue that a true AGI would need to=
 display several different things.</p><p id=3D"ada9" class=3D"pw-post-body-=
paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of =
og oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">First, it would need to h=
ave a capability to communicate with humans in a familiar way =E2=80=94 tha=
t is, through language. Score one for GPT. Most people would agree with thi=
s. However, the next criterion =E2=80=94 which is entirely dependent on the=
 first =E2=80=94 is where the majority of the debate is actually held.</p><=
p id=3D"7b10" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt =
nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-para=
graph=3D"">The Turing Test is the classical defintion of what would define =
a true Aritificial Intelligence. This proposition, posed by the legendary A=
lan Turing in 1950, evaluates an AI based on its ability to display intelli=
gent behavior that is indistinguishable from a human being. Only then, many=
 people say, can we say that a system is truly intelligent.</p><p id=3D"13b=
1" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx=
 ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">=
If you=E2=80=99re anything like me, there is probably a voice in the back o=
f your head, saying =E2=80=9CShould we really evaluate intelligence in this=
 manner? Is human intelligence the only definition of intelligence we shoul=
d consider?=E2=80=9D</p><p id=3D"7001" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm n=
n hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he=
 bl" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Unfortunately, the answer <em class=3D"=
ol">has to be </em><strong class=3D"no hm"><em class=3D"ol">yes</em></stron=
g>.</p><p id=3D"ab40" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq n=
r ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selecta=
ble-paragraph=3D"">What other forms of intelligence do we know? Sure, some =
might say: <em class=3D"ol">=E2=80=9Coh, dolphins show high degrees of inte=
lligence=E2=80=9D</em>, or <em class=3D"ol">=E2=80=9Ccephalopods have been =
shown to have extremely high problem-solving abilities=E2=80=9D</em>, or ev=
en <em class=3D"ol">=E2=80=9Ccertain birds have been shown to plan ahead=E2=
=80=9D</em>. But, while these traits are certainly signs of <em class=3D"ol=
">intelligent behavior</em>, our collective bar for what actually defines <=
em class=3D"ol">intelligence</em> seems to be quite higher.</p><p id=3D"6bd=
9" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx=
 ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">=
And so, we have no choice but to define a true Artificial Intelligence as s=
omething equal to, or better than, ourselves.</p><p id=3D"7b41" class=3D"pw=
-post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob o=
c od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">Now, does GPT =
fall into this category? By itself, it certainly does not, as it is incapab=
le of intelligently communicating with a human. ChatGPT, on the other hand,=
 is a different story.</p><p id=3D"2265" class=3D"pw-post-body-paragraph nm=
 nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc od oe of og oh oi oj =
he bl" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">The best way to think about ChatGPT i=
s to consider that GPT is really the =E2=80=9Cbrain=E2=80=9D behind the who=
le operation. That is, ChatGPT is really the result of teaching GPT how to =
communicate. Imagine if you were born with all of the knowledge you will ev=
er have. Now, imagine if you didn=E2=80=99t have the ability to speak or wr=
ite. How would you get your thoughts across?</p><p id=3D"26e6" class=3D"pw-=
post-body-paragraph nm nn hl no b np nq nr ns nt nu nv nw nx ny nz oa ob oc=
 od oe of og oh oi oj he bl" data-selectable-paragraph=3D"">This is why Cha=
tGPT in particular has changed the game. By teaching GPT how to communicate=
, the engineers at OpenAI have opened up the =E2=80=9Cbrain=E2=80=9D that i=
s GPT, to mere mortals like you and me. From fixing a developer=E2=80=99s c=
ode to helping a student with their homework, ChatGPT has unequivecolly pas=
sed our first test. Now, the only question is: <strong class=3D"no hm"><em =
class=3D"ol">Can it pass the Turing Test?</em></strong></p></div></div></di=
v><div class=3D"ac ci rr rs rt ru" role=3D"separator"><span class=3D"rv by =
bn rw rx ry"></span><span class=3D"rv by bn rw rx ry"></span><span class=3D=
